Retry the error state of a failed delete when the database is lost

`ComputeManager.terminate_instance` (nova/compute/manager.py) sets a server
to ERROR when `_delete_instance` fails with anything but InstanceNotFound:
`_set_instance_obj_error_state` writes vm_state ERROR once and leaves the
task state alone. The `reverts_task_state` decorator then writes
task_state None, also once. On nova-compute both writes are RPC calls to
nova-conductor, which answers a lost database with an
`oslo_messaging.RemoteError` of exc_type DBConnectionError. When the delete
fails while the database is gone, both writes fail and the server keeps the
task state deleting. `API._delete` (nova/compute/api.py) ignores a delete in
that state: every later DELETE answers 204 and does nothing until
nova-compute restarts. Patch 0001 covers only the API's `_local_delete` and
the deletion that `_init_instance` resumes at service start.

A local replay of the random chaos run on #1598 (seed 41807396659) hit
this. Server 68d7e41f-4c07-451e-a7fe-95c6762a2fea was deleted at 16:56:50,
and s07-mariadb-pod-failure started at 16:56:55. `_delete_instance` failed
on nova-compute at 16:58:17 because Neutron was unreachable, and both
cleanup writes failed through nova-conductor: "Setting instance vm_state to
ERROR" at 16:58:17, then "Failed to revert task state for instance. Error:
Remote error: DBConnectionError" at 16:58:21. dizzy's teardown timed out on
the server and exited 1.

Passing `clean_task_state=True` alone would not help, because it is one more
write in the same outage. The error write now clears the task state as well
and goes through `compute_utils.call_with_db_retries`. It retries a
RemoteError of exc_type DBConnectionError with waits that add up to 254
seconds, the bound of `call_cinder_with_retries` from patch 0002 and of
cinder patches 0007 and 0008, which outlasts a database failover. Every
other error, MessagingTimeout included, gets one attempt as before. Once the
write succeeds, the server is ERROR with no task state, its instance fault
records the error `_delete_instance` raised, and the next DELETE reaches
nova-compute again. In the replay that error is itself a DBConnectionError:
`_try_deallocate_network` writes the ERROR state on its own, without a
retry, and that failure replaced Neutron's. When the 254 seconds run out,
the last RemoteError replaces the delete's error and the server keeps
deleting, as it does without the patch.

During an outage a failed delete holds its instance lock and one RPC
executor worker of nova-compute for the 254 seconds of waits plus its 19
writes through nova-conductor. terminate_instance is an RPC cast, so its
caller does not wait. But once failed deletes on one host fill the pool
(executor_thread_pool_size, 64 by default), every other RPC to that
nova-compute queues behind them, and the caller of an RPC call among them
can time out after rpc_response_timeout. The waits therefore stop growing
at 16 seconds, 19 attempts in all: once the database is back, the next
attempt follows within 16 seconds and frees the worker. With the waits of
`call_cinder_with_retries`, which grow to 128 seconds, a write that failed
just before the database came back would wait up to 128 more seconds.

`reverts_task_state` stays as it is. It wraps 33 other methods, whose task
states the API answers with a 409 and which still let a server be deleted,
and six of them are RPC calls whose callers would wait out
rpc_response_timeout.

Tests: CallWithDBRetriesTestCase (nova/tests/unit/compute/test_utils.py)
covers the helper. The new
test_terminate_instance_retries_error_state_after_lost_database fails
without the patch. It records the state each write persists, so it also
fails without `clean_task_state=True`.
test_terminate_instance_error_state_gives_up_after_lost_database pins an
outage longer than the retries: the database's error reaches the caller
and the instance fault. test_terminate_instance_error_state_instance_gone
pins the InstanceNotFound path, which the patch keeps. The upstream tests
of a failed delete in nova/tests/unit/compute/test_compute.py,
test_run_terminate_deallocate_net_failure_sets_error_state,
test_instance_termination_exception_sets_error, test_quotas_failed_delete
and the terminate_instance entry of test_state_revert, assert ERROR or a
cleared task state, which still holds, and pass unchanged. No upstream test
pins the arguments of this `_set_instance_obj_error_state` call.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_nova.sh asserts in the built image that
a delete whose first error write meets a lost database ends in ERROR with no
task state and raises the delete's own error.

Applies to nova 33.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

